# Writing a rhythm on paper: word sounds to notes, a bar of four beats built from crotchets, minims
# and quavers, and every bar that fits a rule. The on-screen bar builder (sound.md) comes later.
lesson music-writing-a-rhythm v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=1 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Writing a rhythm"
  goal "Write a bar of four beats from crotchets, minims and quavers, and clap it."

  look {
    scene 22x20 {
      row top space=2 align=start at=canvas(1, 1) {
        bandstand stand posts=5 notes=3
        drum d lugs=5
      }
      staff bar notes=[1, 0.5, 0.5, 2] below=top gap=1
      text words "pear, ap-ple, boom" below=bar gap=1
    }
    say """
      Ana writes rhythms for Ben's drum at the bandstand. A crotchet is one beat, like pear. Two
      quavers share one beat, like ap-ple. A minim lasts two beats, like a long boom. A bar holds
      four beats, so pear, ap-ple, boom makes one bar.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.rhythm-word-note count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=5 hard-seed=2
    practice music.rhythm-words-to-bar count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=8 hard-seed=12
    level easy {
      worked music.rhythm-finish-bar k=3 level=medium
    }
    practice music.rhythm-finish-bar count=1 seed=1
    practice music.rhythm-quaver-count count=1 seed=1
    practice music.rhythm-too-many-bar count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=5 hard-seed=2
    show music.rhythm-clap-bar
    level hard {
      show music.rhythm-which-is-right s=1 r=0
    }
    practice music.bandstand-drum-quavers count=1 seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.rhythm-which-is-right s=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.rhythm-count-bars k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
  }
  remember {
    say "A crotchet is one beat, two quavers share a beat, and a minim is two."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a rhythm can be written down from its sounds: one sound in a beat is a
    crotchet, two quick sounds are two quavers, and a sound held for two beats is a minim. The wrong
    idea to watch for is giving every sound a whole beat, which turns ap-ple into two crotchets and
    makes a bar too long. In the working, look for the child saying the words and counting the beats
    on their fingers before choosing. The two-star question is that mistake written out; the
    three-star question lists every bar that fits a rule, which is easiest written as letters. A
    child who is stuck can clap the words first and then point at the notes, and go back to the way
    in; stay on this lesson another day if the pairs of quavers still count as two beats. Clap each
    bar together before the child answers, since the hearing has to come from the room. The
    on-screen bar builder comes later; on paper the child chooses and counts. Off the page, make up
    a bar from the names of things in the kitchen and clap it back and forth.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the word to match is pear, one sound, the bars to find have the pair of quavers third and then second, a bar with three beats written is finished once in the teacher's pen before one with two beats left, the quavers fill a bar of four after a crotchet, the drum bar is played twice and its beats counted, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star's bars have three beats and one pair of quavers."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the word to match is boom, held for two beats, the bar to finish starts with quavers, the quavers fill a bar of three beats, a second pair of written bars is judged before the two-star, the drum bar is played three times and its quavers counted, and the three-star's bars have a minim and two beats that can each be a crotchet or a pair of quavers."
  }
}
